From 66f99beb08bd2a78330bfb96fecb015bf3278005 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:25:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 16/24] perf(debug): split a long frame into script,
 style/layout and render in the HUD
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The frame-time percentiles from the phone now say the throughput problem is
mostly gone - a wall reading came back at 59.5 fps with p50 = 11.1 ms on a 90 Hz
panel - but the same screenshot has p99 = 77.5 ms, a 155 ms worst frame, 2.8 %
jank and 19 long tasks in 15 s. Those are hitches, and `longtask` cannot say
whether a hitch was React, a forced reflow or paint. On a phone with no adb that
unknown cannot be resolved by looking, so the HUD has to be the instrument.

`frameSamples.ts` now observes `long-animation-frame` alongside `longtask` and
keeps the breakdown of the slowest frame: script time, style+layout time, render
time, the layout the page forced from inside a script, and the name of the
slowest script. The reduction is a pure exported function
(`summarizeLongAnimationFrame`) covered by test, because the entry mixes absolute
markers (`startTime`, `renderStart`, `styleAndLayoutStart`) with a duration and
the end of the frame is easy to get wrong. The HUD shows `loaf`,
`js·css·paint`, `slowest js`, and `forced layout` when it is non-zero.

A second observer, not a second entry type on the first: engines without the
entry type must still report long tasks, and the HUD already degrades to `loaf:
n/a` when neither arrives.
